%S A402267 6,10,12,14,15,18,20,21,22,24,26,28,30,33,34,35,36,38,39,40,42,44,46,
%T A402267 48,50,51,52,54,55,56,57,58,62,63,65,66,68,69,70,74,75,76,77,80,82,84,
%U A402267 85,86,87,91,92,93,94,95,96,98,99,100,104,105,106,108,111,112
%N A402267 Composite numbers k such that min_{1<j<=k/2} gcd(k, binomial(k,j)) = k/A034699(k).
%C A402267 Let f(k) = min_{1<j<=k/2} gcd(k, binomial(k,j)), and let Q(k) = A034699(k) be the largest prime power dividing k. Erdős and Szekeres observed that f(k) <= k/Q(k) whenever k is not a prime power, by taking j = Q(k). This sequence lists the composite k for which equality holds. Erdős and Szekeres asked for a characterization of these numbers (Erdős Problem 700, part (a)).
%C A402267 No prime power is a term: for e >= 2, f(p^e) = p > 1 = p^e/Q(p^e). On the other hand, every product p*q of two distinct primes is a term (Erdős and Szekeres).
%C A402267 Erdős and Szekeres used P(n) to mean "the greatest prime power which divides n". The erdosproblems.com page instead reads P(n) as the largest prime factor, A006530(n), and that reading gives a different set. For example, 12 is a term here, but 12/A006530(12) = 4 > f(12) = 3.
%C A402267 There are 594 terms <= 10^3, 5493 terms <= 10^4, and 50841 terms <= 10^5.
%H A402267 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="/A402267/b402267.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A402267 Thomas F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/700">Erdős Problem 700</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A402267 Paul Erdős and George Szekeres, <a href="https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1978-46.pdf">Some number theoretic problems on binomial coefficients</a>, Austral. Math. Soc. Gaz. 5 (1978), 97-99.
%e A402267 12 is a term: gcd(12, binomial(12,j)) for j = 2..6 is 6, 4, 3, 12, 12; the minimum 3 equals 12/4 = 12/A034699(12).
%e A402267 45 is not a term: gcd(45, binomial(45,15)) = 3 < 5 = 45/9 = 45/A034699(45).
%o A402267 (PARI) f700(n) = vecmin(vector(n\2-1, j, gcd(n, binomial(n, j+1))));
%o A402267 isok(n) = if(n<4 || isprime(n), 0, my(fa=factor(n)); f700(n) == n/vecmax(vector(#fa~, i, fa[i,1]^fa[i,2])));
%o A402267 (Python)
%o A402267 from math import gcd, comb
%o A402267 from sympy import factorint, isprime
%o A402267 def f(n): return min(gcd(n, comb(n, j)) for j in range(2, n//2+1))
%o A402267 def ok(n): return n > 3 and not isprime(n) and f(n) == n//max(p**e for p, e in factorint(n).items())
%o A402267 print([n for n in range(1, 101) if ok(n)])
